<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sati Beg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sati Beg was a Mongol princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, a daughter of Öljaitü and sister of Abu Sa'id. She was married first to the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chupan">Chupan</a> and later to Arpa Ke'un. After central authority collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-kuchak">Hasan Kuchak</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chupanids">Chupanids</a> raised her to the throne in 1338, making her the only woman to rule the Ilkhanate in her own name, before replacing her in 1339 with Sulayman Khan, whom she then married.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sati-beg.jpg" alt="Sati Beg" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 14th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27nis_al-ahrar_Frontipiece,_1341.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
